f 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308071 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac7f0eca-a730-5c07-b583-54e35d8a27c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308132 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2bf77fe-e98b-5c46-8689-87adeb1cc126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308142 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308142 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308142 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308142 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c6fee208-1362-50e3-a874-bbd81b14b56a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308179 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2e1fabc-7709-598b-9c41-1538cd4e10a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da793013-b50c-5167-aa63-ac5232b9667e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14229489-2d69-5bd0-a160-7cf2fe7048a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "862cb215-e1c8-5e4c-a0a2-cda6cb44f797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b1c05ae-5800-59fe-b52e-f6754d3810af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d942a907-7558-5547-92ea-8c390f2de7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a80f849-223a-5334-b5f3-cfbe5ce70736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308618 #edb/ref 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-{y}^{3})+({x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.5{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.\n$$\n\n Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}{1.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.8 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.771\\,429 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.770\\,917\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.77,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aaeaee6-5815-57be-985f-e23b60c35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312340 #edb/ref 1080 "6.00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312340 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045