d "2203e95e-8ae6-5711-8426-24e1b1e66cba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326916 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330309 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330309 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "284460a7-ef2f-5e83-ad64-00afa46e4e6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330375 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3076ab0c-1b37-5f71-b286-4699046008e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331573 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331573 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331573 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b809f2b1-70a7-515d-988b-6b26b874fb8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331625 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331625 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331625 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd8f61b9-f0da-5836-a896-faa2323ac688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331921 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "df672869-7677-5995-adde-4a3af68231d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186331921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332599 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e167b2e2-f96b-546e-8b81-80e30eadd001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1080 "2-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b28773f-c3b2-5ec9-87f0-8f8e127eca1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" #edb/ref 17592