570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186342732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c171024e-4b6d-5601-9cbd-861fac1471b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344934 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc4f513d-22f2-5838-ab63-68e9cd6ee512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=6$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20b5574b-0241-5d1f-8b06-4befe90938df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345253 #edb/ref 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =3({(3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-77008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/de/dea7eed9213ddf1bb3293950e2490b120a061e9add1172d3b850ccbc58f90454.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y+x)\\,i+x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f314aa29-173f-5141-b324-f3ce0549aed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345267 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Notice that we can write its equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our curve is a part-ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $1$ respectively. This curve can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y+x)\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2(-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-1+0]-[1-0] \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348536 #edb/ref 1080 "e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348536 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348536 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a7ddc79-cbfd-59b5-9638-d178b71104a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349138 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349138 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349138 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b79160f-37ab-5192-8514-437d9b279188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349145 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349145 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349145 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c3b8f50-8779-5d61-90d1-903764b16400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349218 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{e}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349218 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349218 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "544819eb-b6d1-5a40-bb3a-d34d78eef696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349317 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{align